    "description": "Iranian bar associations are professional institutions whose history is closely connected to struggles over lawyers’ independence and access to legal defence. Understanding them requires distinguishing their claim to professional self-government from judicial oversight and the separate judiciary-linked route into legal practice.",
    "tags": ["Bar associations", "Legal profession", "Right to counsel", "Professional independence", "Iranian law"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:37:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:37:02Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Bar Associations and Lawyers’ Independence in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover the history of Iran’s bar associations, professional self-government, parallel licensing structures, and documented pressures on independent defence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "profession-and-independence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why professional independence matters",
        "content": "A bar association is a professional institution for lawyers, rather than a court or a government ministry. In Iran, the question of who controls the profession has long shaped debates about the ability of lawyers to represent clients independently. The Iran Human Rights Documentation Center’s historical account connects the right to counsel with the institutional arrangements governing lawyers, their associations, and professional discipline. [s1]\n\nThe distinction is practical as well as constitutional. A formal entitlement to legal representation does not settle who may obtain a licence, who can stand for a professional association’s governing board, or who can remove a lawyer from practice. These arrangements affect the space in which defence lawyers work. UN special rapporteurs raised precisely these concerns in a communication to Iran’s government in 2021. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-development",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The independence settlement and its later constraints",
        "content": "The modern independence settlement is associated with Prime Minister Mohammad Mosaddeq. The historical account inspected for this page dates the communication of the independence law to 1953 and its subsequent reaffirmation, with changes, to 1955. It describes the law as recognizing the bar’s separate legal personality and independence. Later political changes repeatedly altered the profession’s ability to govern itself. [s1]\n\nThis history helps explain why Iranian discussions of the bar often focus on independence rather than simply membership. Questions about board elections, licensing, and disciplinary jurisdiction concern the distribution of authority between professional institutions and the judiciary. A claim to independence in founding legislation therefore needs to be assessed alongside subsequent rules and documented practice. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "parallel-structures",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "More than one route into legal practice",
        "content": "The International Bar Association’s Human Rights Institute and the Centre for Supporters of Human Rights describe a parallel judiciary-supervised body created under Article 187 of the Third Development Plan legislation. Its existence means that references to Iranian lawyers should not automatically be treated as references to members of the historically independent bar associations. The report interprets this parallel structure as a constraint on bar autonomy. That interpretation is the report’s assessment, rather than a neutral description of all lawyers’ individual conduct. [s3]\n\nThe same report, published in 2025, documents pressures on lawyers and bar institutions, including restrictive regulation and prosecutions. The earlier UN communication questioned judicial vetting of governing-board candidates and interference with professional regulation. These sources establish serious documented concerns, while differing in date and institutional perspective. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-current-conditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading institutional claims carefully",
        "content": "The central issue is the relationship between professional self-government and the conditions under which lawyers can act for clients. Historical legal recognition, present regulatory powers, and reported interference are separate kinds of evidence. The available research supports this distinction, but a complete contemporary directory of associations, licensing rules, and disciplinary procedures still requires further verification. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
